/** ---- dbml-sharepoint PROBE: A GENERATED FORM HEADER AGAINST ITS CALCULATED COLUMNS ----
 *
 * REVISION: d936c3ec
 *
 * QUESTION: does the form header the build generates for calculated columns show,
 * on every row of a grid of hard cases, the same value the calculated column holds?
 *
 * WHY: the header re-derives each calculated column's formula inline, because a form
 * header cannot read a calculated column reliably. A header that differs from its
 * column on any row is a failed build, not a known limitation. Only a live list can
 * say whether the two agree, so this probe puts the generated header and the
 * calculated columns side by side and prints DISAGREE for each row where they differ.
 * No DISAGREE row is the pass.
 *
 * TWO PASTES, one MODE each:
 *   MODE = 'build'  creates the scratch list, its columns and one item per hard case,
 *       writes the generated header to the default content type, then compares.
 *   MODE = 'read'   finds that list and compares again, refreshing the due-day items
 *       so they sit on today's site-local day.
 * MANUAL STEP, the zone run: the probe cannot change the browser's time zone. Run
 * MODE = 'read' a second time with the operating system or browser set to a zone whose
 * offset differs from the site's (for a Melbourne site, UTC or Los Angeles), then paste
 * again. The browser-zone row says whether the run did differ from the site.
 *
 * The header is generated for ONE site zone and date order (DATA.zone, DATA.dateOrder),
 * which the site must have: regenerate the probe with
 * test/manual/generate_form_header_parity_probe.py for another zone.
 *
 * DEPENDS ON (read back, and voiding what rests on them when they do not hold)
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-list         the scratch list, with this probe's Description
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-columns      every column by internal name and type
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-formatter   the content type's formatter, as generated
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-site-zone    the site's time zone offsets equal the generated ones
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-date-order   the site writes a date in the generated order
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-items        every item with the stored values declared
 *   form.display-form.fixture-parity-header-rendered  each item's Display form shows a header
 *
 * OBSERVES (never asserted)
 *   form.display-form.header-agrees-with-columns  AGREE or DISAGREE per item, header against columns
 *   form.display-form.browser-zone-differs        whether this run's browser zone differs from the site's
 *   form.display-form.observed-apostrophe-literal       an apostrophe inside an expression literal
 *   form.display-form.observed-blank-calculated-date    a calculated date returning an empty string
 *   form.display-form.observed-tostring-of-integer      toString of a computed integer
 *   form.display-form.observed-number-of-now            Number(@now) read as epoch milliseconds
 *   The four observed rows stay open until a maintainer records a verdict on the evidence.
 *
 * HOW TO READ IT: a DISAGREE line names the item, the column and both values. A row
 * that rests on a fixture that did not hold prints VOID. Copy the whole block back.
 *
 * HOW TO RUN: F12 -> Console on a site you own, paste, Enter; it prints its plan and
 * stops. Set CONFIRMED and ALLOW_WRITES to true and paste again. Set CLEANUP to true on a
 * later build run to recycle the previous list first; a same-title list without this
 * probe's Description is never touched.
 */
